> How can I find out which files I committed in this last commit?
**assistant**:
> You can easily check which files were included in your last commit using the following Git commands:
> 
> 1. **Show the last commit details**:
>    ```bash
>    git show --name-only
>    ```
>    This command will display the details of the last commit along with a list of files that were changed, added, or deleted.
> 
> 2. **Or, to see only the file names**:
>    If you only want to see the names of the files without the commit message and diff, you can use:
>    ```bash
>    git diff-tree --no-commit-id --name-only -r HEAD
>    ```
>    This command will output only the names of the files involved in the last commit.
> 
> 3. **Alternatively, use `git log`**:
>    Another way to view the last commit and its changes is by using:
>    ```bash
>    git log -1 --name-only
>    ```
>    This provides the last commit along with the names of the affected files.
> 
> Choose the method that suits you best! Let me know if you need any more help.
